1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,675
The Sikh Prisoner of Outram Road
and the Shrine That Moved Twice

2
00:00:04,675 --> 00:00:08,861
In June 1850 the Straits Times
reported, in a few lines,

3
00:00:08,941 --> 00:00:14,350
that a ship from Calcutta had brought
two State prisoners to Singapore.

4
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:18,017
One of them was Bhai Maharaj
Singh, a Sikh religious

5
00:00:18,097 --> 00:00:21,332
leader who had opposed
British rule in Punjab,

6
00:00:21,412 --> 00:00:25,375
and he died in the jail on
Outram Road six years later.

7
00:00:25,375 --> 00:00:29,340
The place where he was cremated
became a shrine that people of

8
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:33,125
several faiths visited for
decades, and it has since moved

9
00:00:33,205 --> 00:00:37,250
twice to reach the Silat Road
gurdwara, where it stands today.

10
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:40,517
Bhai Maharaj Singh was the head of a Sikh

11
00:00:40,597 --> 00:00:44,025
religious order at Naurangabad, in Punjab.

12
00:00:44,025 --> 00:00:47,732
After the death of Maharaja
Ranjit Singh in 1839,

13
00:00:47,812 --> 00:00:53,374
the Sikh kingdom was weakened by
disputes over the succession, and in the

14
00:00:53,454 --> 00:00:58,400
1840s it fought two wars against
the British East India Company.

15
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:02,497
Sikh accounts describe Maharaj
Singh as a leader of the

16
00:01:02,577 --> 00:01:06,369
resistance to British rule;
the British treated him

17
00:01:06,449 --> 00:01:10,550
as an outlaw and put a reward
of 10,000 rupees on him.

18
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:15,858
He was arrested near Adampur
on the 28th of December 1849,

19
00:01:15,938 --> 00:01:18,725
soon after Punjab was annexed.

20
00:01:18,725 --> 00:01:22,856
The Governor-General, Lord
Dalhousie, decided that he should

21
00:01:22,936 --> 00:01:26,725
be held far from Punjab, and
he was sent to Singapore.

22
00:01:26,725 --> 00:01:30,469
The Straits Times of the 18th
of June 1850 reported that

23
00:01:30,549 --> 00:01:34,225
the ship Mahomed Shaw had
arrived from Calcutta "having

24
00:01:34,305 --> 00:01:37,298
on board two State
prisoners," Maharaj Singh,

25
00:01:37,378 --> 00:01:41,475
whom it called the Gooroo, and
his companion Khurruck Singh.

26
00:01:41,475 --> 00:01:45,419
The paper described them as
the instigators of the killing

27
00:01:45,499 --> 00:01:48,332
of two British officers at Multan in 1848,

28
00:01:48,412 --> 00:01:52,148
a charge that reflected the
British view of the events,

29
00:01:52,228 --> 00:01:57,212
and added that both were "closely
watched, under a strict military guard,

30
00:01:57,292 --> 00:02:00,275
in one of the rooms of
Her Majesty's gaol."

31
00:02:00,275 --> 00:02:03,870
According to the Central Sikh
Gurdwara Board's history,

32
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:07,411
Dalhousie had instructed
the authorities in Singapore

33
00:02:07,491 --> 00:02:11,153
that the prisoner should not
be treated with unnecessary

34
00:02:11,233 --> 00:02:14,975
harshness and should be held
somewhere clean but secure.

35
00:02:14,975 --> 00:02:20,046
He was nevertheless kept in a cell
on the upper floor of the new jail,

36
00:02:20,126 --> 00:02:24,100
about 14 by 15 feet, whose
windows had been walled up.

37
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:26,000
His health failed.

38
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,054
He was almost blind within three
years, suffered from rheumatic

39
00:02:30,134 --> 00:02:33,532
pain in his feet and ankles,
and developed a swelling

40
00:02:33,612 --> 00:02:37,550
of the tongue and neck that
left him unable to eat properly.

41
00:02:37,550 --> 00:02:42,350
He died in the jail on
the 5th of July 1856.

42
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:45,921
The Straits Times of the 8th
of July reported that "the

43
00:02:46,001 --> 00:02:49,770
State prisoner at Singapore,
better known as 'The Gooroo'"

44
00:02:49,850 --> 00:02:53,554
had died "at one o'clock on
Saturday last," and suggested

45
00:02:53,634 --> 00:02:57,550
that his companion might now
be allowed to return to India.

46
00:02:57,550 --> 00:03:02,050
Khurruck Singh was later moved
to Penang, where he also died.

47
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:05,218
According to the gurdwara
board, it was Khurruck

48
00:03:05,298 --> 00:03:08,750
Singh who performed the
cremation, near the prison.

49
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:12,350
The cremation site did not stay anonymous.

50
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:15,826
According to accounts collected
by the Sikh community,

51
00:03:15,906 --> 00:03:20,450
Tamils were the first to mark it
with stones and leave flowers there.

52
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:24,570
Muslims later put up green
flags around it and treated

53
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:28,850
it as a keramat, a holy
grave, and Sikhs came as well.

54
00:03:28,850 --> 00:03:32,673
Many people in Singapore came
to know the man buried there

55
00:03:32,753 --> 00:03:36,642
not as Maharaj Singh but as
"Baba Karam Singh," a name that

56
00:03:36,722 --> 00:03:40,625
may have come from an epithet
meaning a worker of wonders.

57
00:03:40,625 --> 00:03:44,955
In the 1940s the shrine was
moved from the prison area to

58
00:03:45,035 --> 00:03:49,600
a small field on the grounds
of Singapore General Hospital.

59
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:53,829
In 1961 a Sikh police officer
installed a copy of the Guru

60
00:03:53,909 --> 00:03:58,362
Granth Sahib there, and the
number of visitors grew until the

61
00:03:58,442 --> 00:04:02,900
crowds were getting in the way
of the hospital's ambulances.

62
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:08,040
The Sikh community and the government
agreed to move the shrine again,

63
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:11,918
and from the 12th of October
1966 it was transferred

64
00:04:11,998 --> 00:04:15,050
to the grounds of the Silat Road gurdwara,

65
00:04:15,130 --> 00:04:20,425
the temple that the Sikh Police
Contingent had helped to build in 1924.

66
00:04:20,425 --> 00:04:25,401
A memorial building dedicated
to Bhai Maharaj Singh was opened

67
00:04:25,481 --> 00:04:30,864
there on the 23rd of October 1995,
and on the 14th of November 1999

68
00:04:30,944 --> 00:04:36,000
the National Heritage Board
declared the site a historic site.

69
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,103
Where it fits in the bigger story:
The Outram Road prison is gone,

70
00:04:41,183 --> 00:04:44,480
and the few lines the
Straits Times gave to

71
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:48,800
its State prisoners in 1850
and 1856 are easy to miss.

72
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:53,180
What kept this story alive in
Singapore was not the official record

73
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:56,842
but the people who marked a
cremation site with stones,

74
00:04:56,922 --> 00:05:01,835
flags and flowers without always knowing
whose it was, and a community that

75
00:05:01,915 --> 00:05:06,375
carried the shrine with it twice
as the city was rebuilt around it.
